{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target fails to propose a substantive relation because it defines 'verisimilitude' merely as a reduction to a 'methodological commitment' (an attitude or goal) rather than establishing a structural, logical, or mechanistic link between the concept of verisimilitude and the aim of truth. The 'REFUTED IF' clause further undermines the relation by suggesting the concept collapses into inductive probability, which contradicts the stated reduction to a non-probabilistic methodological aim, leaving the endpoints disconnected by any valid dependence, mechanism, or abstraction.","premise":"A substantive relation requires a defined structural or logical link (e.g., dependence, mechanism), not just an equation of a concept with a methodological stance, especially when the refutation condition implies the concept lacks the very formal independence required to sustain that stance.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"92968bc0cf42d965","quote":"Verisimilitude as a Regulative Ideal reduces to the methodological commitment that science aims at truth rather than certainty. This relation is refuted if it can be shown that the concept of 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) cannot be formally defined without implicitly relying on inductive probability..."}],"premises_essential":["77d7e7bf7eb12a8b4fc4bf8679f11aa0964c1bfe45e2760249e71ff643d96e7e"],"successor_question":"Can you define a specific structural dependency or shared mechanism between 'verisimilitude' and 'truth' that remains valid even if verisimilitude relies on inductive probability?","target":"77d7e7bf7eb12a8b4fc4bf8679f11aa0964c1bfe45e2760249e71ff643d96e7e"}]}